 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets deliver committed changes.** Browsers hold a WebSocket connection to our own server and receive committed revisions as they are published. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected: it repeats reads on every browser every five seconds and leaves a lag between a commit and its appearance. A hosted realtime service was rejected: it adds a recurring fee and couples the board's delivery path to a provider's subscription model. The trade accepted is that we operate the connection tier ourselves — connection capacity, reconnection, and catch-up after a disconnection are our work, and the change store must keep revisions replayable so a returning browser can catch up.
